    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">How Do You Avoid Sea Urchins When Swimming in Madeira?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>15 November 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Keep your hands and feet off the rocks. Sea urchins in Madeira live on rocky bottoms, boulders, harbour walls and rock pools, from the shallows down to about 20 metres, so swimming in open water and getting in over pebbles or in water shoes keeps you clear of them.</p>

      <h2>Where do sea urchins live in Madeira?</h2><p>On rock, almost always. A 2001 survey of Madeira's coast recorded four common species. Two smaller ones, the purple <em>Paracentrotus lividus</em> and the black <em>Arbacia lixula</em>, sat mostly between 2 and 6 metres deep. The long-spined black urchin, now known as <em>Diadema africanum</em>, and the violet <em>Sphaerechinus granularis</em> lived deeper, from about 4 to 20 metres.</p><p>The long-spined black one is the urchin to respect. Its spines are long and needle thin, and they snap off in the skin. It was the dominant urchin in that survey, and on parts of the south coast it grazes the rock so hard that it leaves bare bottoms coated in pink crusts of coralline algae, known as urchin barrens. Rough water holds it back, so the survey found fewer urchins and more seaweed on the north and south-west coasts, and the most urchins along the south-east.</p><p>Their numbers swing from year to year. Disease outbreaks killed about 65% of Madeira's long-spined urchins in 2009 and up to 90% in 2018, and each time the population came back. A cove with few urchins one summer can be covered in them the next.</p><h2>Where do swimmers actually step on them?</h2><p>At the edges, nearly every time. Urchins do not swim after you or attack. People get hurt when they put weight on a rock they cannot see. The usual places in Madeira are these:</p><ul><li>Climbing in or out over rocks at a natural pool or a rocky shore</li><li>The bottom rungs of ladders and the walls of sea pools and harbours, where urchins sit in the cracks</li><li>Standing up on a boulder for a rest while snorkelling</li><li>Reaching into a crevice to look at a fish or an octopus</li><li>Rock pools at low tide, where small urchins tuck under ledges</li></ul><p>Pebble beaches are much safer, because urchins need firm rock to cling to. Fajã dos Padres, Ribeira Brava and Ponta do Sol all have beaches of stones and pebbles, though the rocks at either end need the same care as any rocky shore.</p><h2>Is swimming from a boat safer than swimming from the rocks?</h2><p>Yes, by a long way. When you swim from a boat you jump into open water and climb back on board when you are done. Your feet never touch the bottom, and the bottom is where the urchins are.</p><p>That is how swimming works on Chifbay's <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip from Funchal</a>. The boat stops for swims at Fajã dos Padres and again at Ribeira Brava, and if you would rather look down at the water than be in it, there are paddle boards on the <a href="/faja-dos-padres-boat-trip">boat trip to Fajã dos Padres</a>. The skipper picks the spot, so you are not choosing a way in over unknown rocks. Urchins only become a question if you head for the rocks yourself.</p><h2>Do you need water shoes in Madeira?</h2><p>For rocks and pools, yes. For jumping off a boat, no. Thin neoprene water shoes with a firm sole make a spine injury much less likely, though a long spine can still go through a thin sole. They also protect your feet from sharp volcanic rock, which is reason enough to own a pair here.</p><p>Bring your own if you want them, because Chifbay does not supply them. They are worth packing if you plan to visit the natural pools at Porto Moniz or Seixal, swim from the lido ladders in Funchal, or explore rocky coves by kayak. Our <a href="/posts/what-to-pack-for-a-boat-trip-in-madeira">packing list for a boat trip in Madeira</a> has the rest.</p><h2>What should you do if you step on a sea urchin?</h2><p>Stay calm. It hurts a lot at first, but most urchin injuries are minor and heal well. Work through it in this order:</p><ol><li><strong>Get out and sit down.</strong> Look at the wound in good light and count the spines.</li><li><strong>Pull out the spines you can grip.</strong> Use tweezers and pull gently and straight. Do not dig or squeeze, because the spines break easily and can be pushed deeper.</li><li><strong>Soak in hot water.</strong> The mild venom in the spines breaks down with heat. Medical reviews suggest water at about 45 °C for 30 to 90 minutes. Test it first with your other hand so you do not scald skin that is already numb.</li><li><strong>Wash with soap and water</strong> and keep the wound clean and dry afterwards. A normal painkiller from a pharmacy helps.</li><li><strong>Let tiny fragments work out.</strong> Small bits left in the skin often come out on their own within days to a few weeks.</li></ol><p>See a doctor the same day if a spine went in at or near a joint, such as a knuckle, a toe joint or a knee, or if moving the joint hurts badly. Go too if you notice spreading redness, swelling, warmth, pus or fever, if it still hurts after five to seven days, or if your tetanus jab is not up to date. Funchal has health centres and a main hospital, and 112 is the emergency number anywhere in Portugal.</p><h2>Who should take more care?</h2><p>Children scrambling on rocks, first-time snorkellers who may panic and try to stand up, and people with diabetes or poor circulation, for whom any foot wound is more serious. For them the plan is simple: swim from a boat or a pebble beach, wear water shoes near rock, and keep hands out of holes.</p><p>If the thought of urchins puts you off rock swimming altogether, that is a fair choice. Madeira has plenty of places to swim where you never touch the bottom. For the pools and their ladders, see our guide to <a href="/posts/natural-pools-madeira">Madeira's natural pools</a>.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Are sea urchins in Madeira poisonous?</h3><p>Their spines carry a mild venom that causes sharp pain and swelling, but they are not dangerous for most people. The main risks are spines left in a joint and infection.</p><h3>Should you pee on a sea urchin sting?</h3><p>No. It does nothing useful. Remove the spines you can grip, soak the area in hot water and keep it clean.</p><h3>Are there sea urchins at Fajã dos Padres?</h3><p>As anywhere with rock on this coast, the rocky edges and bottom can have urchins. Swimming from the boat into open water keeps you away from them.</p><h3>Do sea urchins move?</h3><p>Slowly, on tiny tube feet. They do not chase or attack anyone. Injuries happen when people put a hand or foot on them.</p>

      <blockquote>Urchins never come to you. Every spine in a foot started with a step onto a rock nobody looked at.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

